# nanoLM

The purpose of this project is to learn about language models from basics

Currently this repo contains training and inference code for BPE based tokenizers inspired by Andrej Karpathy's [Let's build the GPT Tokenizer](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zduSFxRajkE) video.

```
nanoLM/
│
├── tokenizer/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── base.py
│   ├── basic_tokenizer.py
│   ├── inference.py
│   ├── regex_tokenizer.py
│   └── train.py
│
├── utils/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── data_downloader.py
│
└── README.md
```

## Components

1. base.py which is kind of acting as a base for all the tokenizers
2. basic_tokenizer this file implements everything needed to implement a normal tokenizer without considering the pattern i.e. dog., dog?, dog! are being treated as different tokens which is not appropriate
3. regex_tokenizer this one tries to mitigate the issues present in basic one by first chunking the given text based on a regex pattern and then performs merging
4. we have data_downloader.py which is being used as to download [TinyStories Dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories)
5. 

## Setup

clone the repo and install the packages mentioned in requirements.txt

```bash
git clone https://github.com/riteshrm/nanoLM.git
cd nanoLM
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Download Data

```bash
python -m utils.data_downloader # This will download the required dataset
```

## Train

```bash
python -m tokenizer.train --tokenizer_type="regex" --vocab_size=512 --data_path="data/TinyStories" --save_path="tokenizer_model"
```

for tokenizer there are two options basic and regex and vocab size is by default 512 and data_path is where the tinystories has been stored
After training you will find two files named as merges.model and vocab.vocab in the folder specified by Path(save_path, tokenizer_type)

## Inference

```bash
python -m tokenizer.inference --tokenizer_type="regex" --save_path="tokenizer_model/regex"
```
